WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.730
- [Announcer] This Great Big Story

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:03.722
was made possible by Fairmont-

00:00:03.722 --> 00:00:06.283
an ACCOR Hotels experience.

00:00:07.865 --> 00:00:09.140
- A lot of people ask, well, what does

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.010
the Shanghai Restoration Project mean?

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.703
And it's inspired by the
1930s Shanghai jazz scene.

00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:17.840
♪ Do do do do ♪

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.920
Back then, you had musicians

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.590
from all different cultural backgrounds

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.220
that came together to make a music

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.510
that couldn't be attributed

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.680
to any particular culture or nationality.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.675
What we wanted to do was
sort of revive this spirit.

00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:33.800
My name is Dave Liang.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.200
- My name is Sun Yunfan.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.956
- [Dave] We're the Shanghai
Restoration Project.

00:00:37.956 --> 00:00:40.456
♪ Do do do do ♪

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:45.540
The Shanghai Restoration Project

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.450
has been around since 2005.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.710
- We started performing as a duo,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.760
I think the first time, in 2016.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.990
We produced maybe 14, 15 albums.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.590
These are some of the
artworks that I've done

00:00:58.590 --> 00:00:59.868
for our album covers.

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:03.017
- We've played at the
Rio Cinema in London,

00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:05.976
the Concrete &amp; Grass Festival in Shanghai;

00:01:05.976 --> 00:01:07.711
played the Great Wall of china.

00:01:07.711 --> 00:01:10.294
(upbeat music)

00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:22.130
- I grew up in China,
and Dave grew up here

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.490
but to a Chinese immigrant family.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.090
When we first met I would ask him, like,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.010
"Why do you use so many
Chinese instruments?"

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.103
I never listened to Chinese
folk musicians growing up.

00:01:33.103 --> 00:01:36.170
- [Dave] Growing up I listened
to all sorts of genres,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.410
but the one thing that just stuck out

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.690
were these Chinese instruments.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.910
You know, in my home, they were just

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.460
like nothing else that I heard.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.580
- [Sun] I think our merge of culture,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.823
merge of perspectives
also influence our music.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:53.276
(upbeat music)

00:01:53.276 --> 00:01:54.300
- [Dave] What you'll hear

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.460
in our music are harmonic, rhythmical,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.620
melodic influences from
all over the world.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.500
You'll hear sounds from China.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.470
You'll hear sounds from Brazil,

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.480
influences from Senegal or Ethiopia

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.975
or France or Portugal.

00:02:05.975 --> 00:02:08.267
- [Sun] The process of creating

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.649
a new sound is always different.

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:14.326
We use a lot of samples
from every day objects.

00:02:14.326 --> 00:02:18.363
- We tend to go to a lot of random places

00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:21.140
for touring or for travel

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.623
and every place has a very unique sound.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:27.200
- Sometimes when we are on trip,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.960
a sound in the street,
we'll just like take

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.173
our phone or a recorder
to record the sound.

00:02:33.843 --> 00:02:36.680
- We were in London

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:37.694
at the Tate museum

00:02:37.694 --> 00:02:39.411
Yunfan had bought a poster

00:02:39.411 --> 00:02:42.942
and she just started
hitting it against her leg

00:02:42.942 --> 00:02:46.220
and hear it is

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.603
in the song Library Break In.

00:02:48.603 --> 00:02:51.780
(slow bright music)

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.760
I think a lot

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.450
of these unique sounds generally will make

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.500
their way into our music.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.470
The whole idea is that the world is small.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.070
And that sounds are something

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.893
that can bring everybody together.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.760
- [Sun] We feel like today's world

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.730
is much more interconnected.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.179
And living in New York or Shanghai today,

00:03:08.179 --> 00:03:10.554
that's what we see in our lives

00:03:10.554 --> 00:03:13.064
and that reflects in our music.

00:03:13.064 --> 00:03:16.064
(audience cheering)

